WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:04.240
John Rambo has finally met his match: the
critics.

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:08.540
Reviews are coming in for Rambo: Last Blood,
which brings an end to the story begun with

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:10.080
1982's First Blood.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:14.440
The general consensus: Rambo deserved a better
sendoff, and one with much less of a sadistic

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:15.440
streak.

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:17.800
"I'm gonna hurt ya real bad."

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:20.999
Over-the-top carnage is nothing new for this
franchise.

00:00:20.999 --> 00:00:25.130
2008's Rambo similarly turned off critics
with its cartoonish reliance on dishing up

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:27.539
as much blood and guts as humanly possible.

00:00:27.539 --> 00:00:32.160
But Last Blood's detractors — of which there
were many — were taken aback not only by

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:36.460
the extreme level of the violence on display,
but by some of the more troubling themes implied

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:41.400
by the film's plot, which involves Rambo crossing
the border into Mexico to search for a family

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:43.980
friend who has been kidnapped by sex traffickers.

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:47.880
Many of the flick's detractors noted that
political themes had always been present in

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:52.670
the Rambo series, even — perhaps especially
— First Blood, which dealt with the mistreatment

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:55.460
of Vietnam veterans after they returned home
from the war.

00:00:55.460 --> 00:01:00.280
But the series' worldview seems to have changed
with the times, and not for the better, according

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:02.520
to Variety's Peter Debruge, who wrote,

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:07.500
"[Rambo: Last Blood is] another cruel and
ugly showcase of xenophobic carnage squeezed

00:01:07.509 --> 00:01:10.200
into barely 80 minutes and packaged for export.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:14.220
[The screenwriters] adopt the racist view
of Mexicans as murderers, drug dealers and

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:18.490
rapists [...] This character has always been
a mess of contradictions, representing on

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:22.890
one hand the permanent damage that military
service can do to one's soul, while simultaneously

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:25.360
suggesting what the ideal soldier looks like.

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:28.620
Rambo single-handedly wins the wars that America
can't.

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:32.140
And the blood isn't likely to stop here, or
anytime soon."

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:36.719
Tom Glasson of Concrete Playground concurred,
musing that the level of violence presented

00:01:36.719 --> 00:01:42.110
in Last Blood — a step up even from 2008's
Rambo, in his estimation — was better suited

00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:43.700
for a very different type of franchise.

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:44.640
Glasson wrote,

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:49.320
"[Rambo: Last Blood] a film that aspires to
be Logan, yet lands somewhere closer to The

00:01:49.329 --> 00:01:50.979
Texas Chain Saw Massacre.

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:56.480
[Rambo] abhors violence, yet maintains a terrifying
arsenal of knives, guns and explosives.

00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:01.700
And beneath [his] picturesque ranch is a straight-up
house of horrors, physically and psychologically.

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:03.400
But is the film still enjoyable?

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:04.700
Mostly, no."

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:09.080
Yet still, some reviewers were even less kind
— like The Guardian's Peter Bradshaw, who

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:13.019
absolutely leveled the picture in his review's
first paragraph, writing,

00:02:13.019 --> 00:02:17.879
"This massively enlarged prostate of a film
can only make you wince with its badly acted

00:02:17.879 --> 00:02:23.160
geronto-ultraviolence, its Trumpian fantasies
of Mexican rapists and hilariously insecure

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:28.770
US border, and its crass enthusiasm for rape-revenge
attacks undertaken by a still-got-it senior

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:32.400
dude, 73 years young, on behalf of a sweet
teenager."

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:36.640
Even some of the reviewers who recommended
the film seemed aghast at its violence, with

00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:41.260
many remarking that even for a series which
has always hung its hat on wanton bloodshed,

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:43.040
Last Blood is a bridge too far.

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:45.400
Fred Topel of Showbiz Cheat Sheet wrote,

00:02:45.410 --> 00:02:50.110
"It's funny to think how these movies were
always symbols of Hollywood's gratuitous violence.

00:02:50.110 --> 00:02:53.890
They were bloody, but blood was the worst
they could show in the '80s.

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:57.490
[...] Last Blood takes violence so far it
almost has to be the last blood because there's

00:02:57.490 --> 00:02:58.820
no more blood left.

00:02:58.820 --> 00:03:02.880
Remember when the most graphic scene in First
Blood was when he cleaned his arm wound with

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:03.900
his knife?"

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:08.799
Again, this was a positive review, which indicates
that even some of those predisposed to expect

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:13.100
a heaping helping of bloody mayhem got a little
bit more than they bargained for.

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:17.580
Other critics posting positive reviews seemed
to recommend the flick in spite of themselves.

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:21.840
Vince Mancini of Uproxx, for example, penned
an assessment which read for all the world

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:25.689
like an absolute evisceration of the film,
right up until the point where the critic

00:03:25.689 --> 00:03:27.080
offered his endorsement.

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:32.360
"Trump-era Rambo is essentially a human border
wall keeping out Mexican rapists and murderers.

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:36.600
[...] It's so genuinely horrific I'm convinced
there are real-life cartel videos celebrating

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:38.659
the torture of rivals that are less gory.

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:43.120
I was either howling with nervous laughter
or covering my face the entire time.

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:46.020
[...] This movie is the product of truly deranged
minds.

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:47.340
It's a must-see."

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:51.500
One thing viewers shouldn't expect is for
the film to do justice to its iconic main

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:52.380
character.

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:57.200
This idea was summed up most succinctly by
Harry Guerin of RTE, who wrote,

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:01.880
"This last post for the 37-year-old franchise
is a cheap and rushed send-off.

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:06.000
There are plenty of bodies but not much soul
[…] and although this is by far the bloodiest

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:09.100
of Rambo's rampages, it's also the least cathartic.

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:10.640
Don't remember him this way."

